{"type":"text","timestamp":1787395940603,"sessionID":"ses_fd6e692ddffeGvGNwVSLJa3nH7","part":{"id":"prt_0291997df001mPy7K4187zZo0Y","messageID":"msg_029199334001e0UDHHvN6qH7WB","sessionID":"ses_fd6e692ddffeGvGNwVSLJa3nH7","type":"text","text":"Aha! Line 4 has `n when n < 5` — the pattern `n` introduces the binding, and then the guard `n < 5` tries to use that same name. This is **rebinding**! The spec says in §2:\n\n> \"a head that repeats a bare name — `F(acc, acc)` — is an **error**, not an equality constraint: both are introductions, and the second rebinds what the first bound\"\n\nSo in `n when n < 5`, the first `n` is an introduction, and the `n` in the guard is a reference to a name being used **before it's fully bound**. Let me check the other failing held-out cases:","time":{"start":1787395938271,"end":1787395940597}}}
